# =============================================================================
# apps/api — example environment. Copy to .env.local and adjust LOCAL values.
#
#     cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env.local
#
# Never commit .env.local. Never put a production value here.
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS LOADED. Next.js reads `.env.local` from the APPLICATION
# directory it is given, and the launcher (scripts/dev/start-local.mjs) starts
# the API as `next dev apps/api`. So this file's sibling `.env.local` — not the
# repository-root one — is what the API process sees. The root `.env.local` is
# read only by docker-compose.yml's `env_file`.
#
# THE AUTHORITATIVE CONTRACT IS THE CODE, in two files:
#   - src/server/config/backend-config.ts — the full schema, every entry below
#   - src/config/env.ts                   — the older Supabase/NODE_ENV subset
# Names below, values yours. `scripts/ci/check-env-contract.mjs` fails the build
# when a name either schema reads is missing from this file or the root one.
#
# A COMPLETE PROSE INVENTORY — consumer, purpose, who must supply the value —
# lives in docs/platform/environment-configuration.md.
#
# `REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION` below means exactly one thing, and it is checked:
# `productionConfigurationProblems()` in backend-config.ts lists the name when
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV is `staging` or `production` and the value is absent, and
# readiness then reports `configuration.production-required` as failing. Nothing
# on that list is required locally.
#
# NO DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENT EXISTS TODAY. Local is the only one (ADR-012).
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BROWSER-SAFE (NEXT_PUBLIC_*)
#
# Inlined into the bundle by Next at BUILD time, and therefore public. Never
# give this prefix to a secret — above all not to the service-role key.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Local Supabase stack (supabase start). Read by src/config/env.ts and handed to
# the identity adapter by src/modules/iam/index.ts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321

# The anon key printed by `supabase status`. Public BY DESIGN — its safety is
# Row-Level Security, which is implemented and forced on every tenant table.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=replace-with-local-anon-key

# Which environment this deployment is. local | development | staging | production
# (apps/web/src/lib/env.ts uses a DIFFERENT vocabulary for its own copy of this
# name: local | preview | production. The two are not interchangeable.)
#
# It is load-bearing three times over: it is the first segment of every storage
# key and those are immutable once written; it selects bucket auto-creation; and
# it is what switches the production-required check on.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV=local

# Build identity, surfaced by /api/health. Injected at image build; defaults to
# 0.1.0 and `unknown` (src/shared/constants/app.ts).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_VERSION=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_COMMIT_SHA=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER-ONLY — never expose, never log, never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Server log verbosity: debug | info | warn | error. Unset or unrecognised falls
# back to the built-in default (warn under test, info otherwise).
# Read by src/server/observability/logger.ts.
# LOG_LEVEL=

# Bypasses RLS entirely. The local value comes from `supabase status`; the Owner
# acceptance helper (scripts/dev/owner-acceptance/create-owner-account.mjs)
# writes it into .env.local for you. REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION — src/modules/iam
# refuses to compose the identity provider without it.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE
#
# No example URL appears anywhere in this file on purpose: the tracked-secrets
# gate rejects credential-shaped URLs even as stand-ins, and it is right to.
# Each value below is a PostgreSQL connection string; compose it from what
# `supabase status` prints (host 127.0.0.1, port 54322, database postgres) with
# the login role and password for the archetype named.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The request path's pool. Its login role must be a member of app_runtime and
# must NOT hold BYPASSRLS — readiness refuses a BYPASSRLS role outright.
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION.
# DATABASE_URL=

# The control plane. Its login role holds app_platform and NO other application
# archetype; it deliberately has no fallback to DATABASE_URL, so the platform
# path fails closed when it is absent. REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION.
# PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL=

# The outbox worker. Falls back to DATABASE_URL when unset. The worker role is
# all-tenant by design, which is exactly why the request path must not use it.
# WORKER_DATABASE_URL=

# RESERVED — accepted and read by NOTHING. Setting it has no effect.
# poolFor('replica') returns the primary pool and no replica is provisioned
# (ADR-012). Listed in RESERVED_SETTINGS in
# apps/api/src/server/config/backend-config.ts.
# DATABASE_REPLICA_URL=

# Pool and timeout shape. Every one has a default and a hard upper bound.
# DB_POOL_MAX=10                   # 1..50
# DB_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000    # 1000..300000
# DB_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=5000    # 500..60000
# DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000    # 100..120000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OUTBOX WORKER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OUTBOX_BATCH_SIZE=25             # 1..500
# OUTBOX_MAX_CONCURRENCY=4         # 1..32
# OUTBOX_LEASE_SECONDS=300         # 5..3600
# OUTBOX_MAX_ATTEMPTS=8            # 1..50
# OUTBOX_BASE_BACKOFF_MS=1000      # 10..600000
# OUTBOX_MAX_BACKOFF_MS=300000     # 1000..3600000
# OUTBOX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=2000     # 50..600000
# OUTBOX_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=15000   # 0..120000

# Identity of this worker process, recorded as the queue claimant.
# Must match ^[a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{1,62}$.
# WORKER_ID=outbox_worker

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTP EDGE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Comma-separated EXACT remote addresses of reverse proxies that may be trusted
# to set X-Forwarded-For. EMPTY BY DEFAULT, and empty is the only safe default:
# trusting the header unconditionally lets any caller choose its own rate-limit
# identity. Set it to the addresses of your own proxies and nothing else.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=

# RESERVED — accepted and read by NOTHING. Setting it has no effect and no
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin header is emitted for it: there is no CORS layer
# and no response-header layer on this tier. It is NOT required in production,
# because refusing readiness over a value that governs nothing would be a false
# gate. Listed in RESERVED_SETTINGS in
# apps/api/src/server/config/backend-config.ts.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Rate limiting: true | false. MUST NOT be `false` in production — it is the
# only protection the public routes have, and the production check lists it.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true

# In-process cache bounds.
#
# CACHE_DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS is RESERVED — accepted and read by NOTHING. Setting
# it has no effect: Cache.set() requires an explicit TTL from its caller and no
# path falls back to a default. Listed in RESERVED_SETTINGS in
# apps/api/src/server/config/backend-config.ts.
# CACHE_DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS=60     # 1..86400 — reserved, no effect
# CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES=5000           # 16..100000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTHENTICATION (ADR-019)
#
# Every value here is server-only and is never echoed. For a hosted Supabase
# project these come from that project's own settings; locally the Owner
# acceptance helper writes the secret and the issuer for you.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Value written to and matched against iam.user_accounts.identity_provider.
# Must match ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{1,62}$.
# AUTH_IDENTITY_PROVIDER=supabase

# Expected `iss`. A token minted by any other issuer is rejected outright.
# For a Supabase project this is the project URL with /auth/v1 appended.
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION.
# AUTH_JWT_ISSUER=

# Expected `aud`. GoTrue issues `authenticated` for a signed-in principal.
# AUTH_JWT_AUDIENCE=authenticated

# Accepted signing algorithms, comma-separated. An allow-list rather than
# "whatever the token's header says" is what stops `alg: none` and the
# RS256-to-HS256 confusion attack. Widen it only deliberately.
# AUTH_JWT_ALGORITHMS=HS256

# Shared secret for HS* verification. Absent means the application cannot
# authenticate anyone at all. REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION.
# AUTH_JWT_SECRET=

# Tolerated clock drift between the provider and this process, in seconds.
# Bounded 0..300: an unbounded skew turns `exp` into a suggestion.
# AUTH_CLOCK_SKEW_SECONDS=60

# Session lifetime controls.
# SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=30       # 1..1440, measured from last activity
# SESSION_ACTIVITY_REFRESH_SECONDS=60   # 5..3600, how stale last_seen_at may get

# Comma-separated EXACT, ABSOLUTE redirect destinations permitted for
# password-reset and invitation links. EMPTY BY DEFAULT, and an empty list
# rejects every caller-supplied redirect — forwarding an arbitrary redirect to
# the provider is an open redirect carrying a single-use token.
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION: name the public web origin's reset and invitation
# URLs, or neither flow can complete.
# AUTH_REDIRECT_ALLOWLIST=

# Account lockout thresholds.
# LOGIN_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS=5      # 1..100 consecutive failures
# LOGIN_FAILURE_WINDOW_MINUTES=15  # 1..1440

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OBJECT STORAGE
#
# No production object store is provisioned (ADR-012). `unconfigured` is the
# default and refuses to sign a URL rather than issuing one that leads nowhere.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Adapter selection. Must match ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{1,62}$. Three are implemented:
#   unconfigured  — refuses every call (default)
#   local_fake    — deterministic, in-process, signs against a .invalid host
#   s3_compatible — real S3 protocol; local Supabase speaks it
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION: neither `unconfigured` nor `local_fake` can serve a
# deployed attachment request, and attachments are not feature-gated.
# For local use, `npm run dev:up` resolves the S3 values from the running stack
# and passes them to the API tier, so you rarely set these by hand.
# STORAGE_PROVIDER=s3_compatible
# STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:54321/storage/v1/s3
# STORAGE_S3_REGION=local
# STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# STORAGE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# STORAGE_BUCKET=rootlco-attachments

# Signed-URL lifetimes, bounded on BOTH sides: a URL that lives for hours is a
# bearer credential in a browser history; one that lives for two seconds cannot
# survive a slow upload.
# STORAGE_UPLOAD_URL_TTL_SECONDS=600     # 30..900
# STORAGE_DOWNLOAD_URL_TTL_SECONDS=120   # 15..600

# Platform CEILING on one object, in bytes (default 25 MiB, max 256 MiB).
# shared.document_categories.max_size_bytes stays authoritative per category.
# STORAGE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=26214400

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MESSAGE DELIVERY
#
# `unconfigured` refuses to deliver. Enqueueing still works, because the
# outbound row is the durable record and delivery is the worker's concern.
# NO DELIVERY PROVIDER IS IMPLEMENTED — there is no adapter to select yet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Must match ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{1,62}$.
# NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER=unconfigured
# NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_MS=5000    # 100..60000, per attempt
# NOTIFICATION_MAX_RENDERED_CHARS=20000    # 256..262144, across every channel

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPERATIONAL BUDGETS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Budget for the whole readiness probe, in milliseconds (50..10000). A probe
# that can block indefinitely turns a slow dependency into a health-check outage.
# READINESS_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

# Largest row estimate an export authorization will approve synchronously.
# EXPORT_MAX_ROWS=50000            # 1..1000000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROTATION EXPECTATIONS
#
# - Local keys are generated by the Supabase CLI, are not secret between
#   developers, and are worthless outside your machine.
# - Any NON-LOCAL credential must be rotated: on suspected exposure immediately;
#   when a person with access leaves, immediately; otherwise on a schedule the
#   owners set.
# - If a real secret is ever committed: ROTATE FIRST, then purge history. Do not
#   re-post the value anywhere — not in an issue, a PR, a log, or a report.
# - Reporting channel: see SECURITY.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
